## 1. Introduction
Zeolites are one kind of crystalline inorganic material and have been widely used as adsorbents and catalysts in many important fields.1–4 The basic structural feature of each zeolite framework is the presence of the tetrahedral \(\mathrm{TO}_{4}\) \(\mathrm{T}=\mathrm{Si}^{4+}\) , \({\sf A l}^{3+}\) , \(\mathsf{P}^{5+}\) , etc.) node and the T–O–T linker at an average angle of about \(145^{\circ}\) . Each \(\mathrm{TO}_{4}\) tetrahedron is connected with four neighbors by sharing their vertex O atoms to form a three-dimensional (3D) four-connected zeotype framework. Commonly, organic amines are used as structure-directing agents (SDAs) to construct zeolites with different topologies.5 Now, over 230 distinct
framework types have been identified by the Structure Commission of the International Zeolite Association (IZA-SC).6,7 Considering the important applications of zeolites, researchers have never stopped the pursuit of novel zeolites with desired framework structures.
Recently, the search for new zeolite-like structures was extended to metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers (PCPs).8–16 The current goal is to find new zeolite-like materials from the metal–organic coordination approach. MOFs are a new class of crystalline porous materials consisting of metal ions or clusters coordinated to organic ligands, and usually contain potential voids.11 MOFs have received extensive attention not only for their fascinating structural features, such as permanent porosity, tunable porous size/shape, high surface area, and uniform open cavities, but also for their potential applications in gas adsorption/separation, catalysis, and so on.17–19 Metal–organic
