zeolites (MOZs) are one kind of 4-connected metal–organic frameworks with permanent porosity and high stability. As a subclass of MOFs and metal–organic analogues of zeolites, MOZs integrate the merits of both MOFs and zeolites, for example, the high surface area and porosity as well as the exceptional stability of zeolites, and have become one of the most active fields in MOFs.
In this context, given the high demand for new porous materials in industrial and social economy, the exploration of new porous materials, like MOFs with expanding zeolitic networks and multi-functionalization, so called MOZs, has become an encouraging avenue. Hence, this review focuses on the feasibility of various design strategies and the elucidation of structure–property relationships (Fig. 1), delineating an architectural blueprint toward the designed synthesis of MOZs:
(i) obeying the charge matching principle based on tetrahedral centers with various charge states for constructing stable MOZs;
(ii) introducing functional groups in MOZs for extensive applications;
(iii) introducing catalytically active \(\mathrm{TO}_{4}\) sites of zeolites into ZIF frameworks to fill the gap between zeolites and ZIFs;
(iv) using enlarged tetrahedral building units, such as \(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}\) and metal–oxygen clusters, as nodes to expand zeolitic networks;
(v) expanding the components of MOZs from the traditional imidazole-linked ligands to organic carboxylate ligands to diversify the structures of MOZs (Scheme 1).
Each strategy is mainly discussed based on recent advances in MOZs with some prominent examples. The concluding outlook will summarize the advancements in this field, with an emphasis on the potential of pertinent applications (Table 1).
## 2. Charge matching principle of metal–organic zeolites
Inspired from zeolite structures with over 230 topologies only composed of tetrahedral \(\mathrm{TO}_{4}\) units and \(\upmu_{2}\)-bridging O atoms, these simplest two-component compounds composed of tetrahedral coordinated metal ions and \(\upmu_{2}\)-bridging ligands are the most reliable system for MOZs. Five-membered imidazole (Im),
