as the shortest and simplest organic ligand, can easily bind two metal ions through its N atoms as a strong ligating donor after deprotonation. It is noticed that the \(\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{Im}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{M}\) ( \(\mathbf{M}=\) metal ions) angle of \(145^{\circ}\) is coincident with the Si–O–Si angle in many zeolites (Scheme 2). Thus, the incorporation of deprotonated Im derivatives and tetrahedral coordinated metal ions makes it possible to prepare MOZs.
It is well-known that there are many elements with tetrahedral coordination geometry \((\mathrm{Li}^{+},\,\mathrm{Co}^{+},\,\mathrm{Ag}^{+},\,\mathrm{Mg}^{2+},\,\mathrm{Zn}^{2+},\,\mathrm{Co}^{2+},\,\mathrm{Cd}^{2+},\,\mathrm{B}^{3+},\,etc.)\) in the periodic table. So these elements might be the choice to act as tetrahedral nodes. For the organic linkers, there are a series of Im derivatives. We can use simple or mixed imidazolates to bridge the tetrahedral centers. Besides coordination geometries, global and local charge matching is also needed to be analyzed for the preparation of MOZs. The global charge matching refers to the proper host–guest charge balance, while the local charge matching refers to the proper distribution of cationic species around the anionic linkers with a negative charge.
In the imidazolate system, the self-assembly of Im ligands and tetrahedral centers (M) with different charge will generate 4-connected frameworks with the formula \(\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}\). Here the charge matching principle is that the valence sum (vs) from 4-connected cationic nodes to the anionic species is generally not higher than or significantly lower than the valence of the anion. The vs is calculated by applying Pauling's second rule (the electrostatic valence rule) or Brown's valence sum rule. For example, linkages \(\mathbf{M}^{+}\mathbf{-Im-M}^{+}\) (vs = 0.5, too low) and \(\mathbf{M}^{3+}\mathbf{-Im-M}^{3+}\) (vs = 1.5, too high) are not formed, while linkages such as \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\mathbf{-Im-M}^{2+}\) (vs = 1), \(\mathbf{M}^{+}\mathbf{-Im-M}^{2+}\) (vs = 0.75), \(\mathbf{M}^{+}\mathbf{-Im-M}^{3+}\) (vs = 1) and \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\mathbf{-Im-M}^{3+}\) (vs = 1.25) are allowed. Based on this principle, the following series of MOZs with different charged tetrahedral centers have been successfully developed.
## 2.1 ZIFs based on the \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\) tetrahedral center
For MOZs, the most widely used are the positive 2 charged tetrahedral metals \((\mathrm{Zn}^{2+},\ \mathrm{Co}^{2+},\ \mathrm{Co}^{2+},\ \mathrm{Mg}^{2+},\ etc.)\), which are connected through ditopic imidazolate with linkages \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\mathbf{-Im-M}^{2+}\) (vs = 1) to a neutral framework (Scheme 2a). As an exemplar of MOZs, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are developed based on this strategy. Because of their exceptional stability, high surface area and porosity, ZIF materials exhibit excellent performance in gas storage/separation, catalysis, drug transport and many other fields. Such areas can be seen in detail in other reviews.
Very recently, Prof. Yaghi and co-workers proposed that the topological variety of ZIFs could be realized via the link–link interactions. The term link–link interactions is inclusive of all the potential interactions that may occur between the organic links in solution, such as hydrogen bonding, \(\pi\cdot\cdot\pi\) attractions and steric interference. Especially, the kind of substituent groups and their position in the Im rings have a huge effect on the final topologies of ZIFs. These effects can be evaluated by the steric index \((\delta)\). A high \(\delta\) could be potentially achieved by introducing bulky substrates in the Im linkers at a specific position. Accordingly, they prepared a series of ZIFs which have
the largest pore opening \((22.5\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) and the largest cage size \((45.8\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) known for all porous tetrahedral structures (Fig. 2a).
Using organic molecules as SDAs or templates is an alternative synthetic strategy for the exploration of ZIFs with large pore opening and high porosity. Prof. Dong and co-workers synthesized two new ZIFs with CAN and AFI topologies (denoted as \(\mathrm{CAN}[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}]\) and \(\mathrm{AFI}[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}]\)) with 12-membered ring pore opening by using bulky amides, namely, dibutylformamide (DBF) and dipropylformamide (DPF), as solvent and SDAs.37 In addition, some interesting ZIFs can be realized in their large-scale synthesis for practical application. For example, RHO-type \(\left[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{eim})_{2}\right]\) (MAF-6) with large cage and channel size was first reported as the byproduct of MAF-5.54 Another MER-type \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}\) (ZIF-10) had the highest porosity \((\approx\!61\%)\) among all known \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}\) frameworks and the lowest density \((0.75\;\mathrm{g\;cm}^{-3})\) among all ZIFs; however, it was first reported as solvated individual crystals isolated from the reaction of \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) and Im in \(N_{x}N^{\prime}\)-dimethylformamide (DMF) and was hard to reproduce.58 Prof. Chen and Zhang reported the rapid solution synthesis of RHO-type MAF-6 by using cyclohexane as the SDA.38 MAF-6 with high crystallinity, purity and thermal/chemical stability can be synthesized in large quantity. Prof. Holman reported the reproducible synthesis of MER-type ZIF-10 \(\left(\mathrm{MeMeCH}_{2}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}\right)\) by employing a simple macrocyclic solute, \(\mathrm{MeMeCH}_{2}\), as a kinetic template (Fig. 2b). Phase-pure MER-MeMeCH2@ZIF-10 was easily activated and exhibited high porosity.39
Considering the high surface area and stability of ZIFs, recent studies indicated that ZIFs as gas sensors may be promising candidates for detecting harmful gases.66–68 Zhang's group reported that ZIF-67 supported by Ag-Pd interdigitated electrodes exhibited excellent selectivity and sensitivity to formaldehyde vapor at a relatively low operating temperature of \(150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).40 The ZIF-67 sensor had been demonstrated to possess low formaldehyde detection limit \((5\,\mathrm{ppm})\), fast response and recovery time, and good linearity between concentration and sensitivity in the low concentration range \((5{-}50\;\mathrm{ppm})\), suggesting its potential application for formaldehyde detection. They also synthesized zni-type \(\left[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}\right]_{n}\) and employed it as gas sensor for detecting trimethylamine (TMA), which is usually generated during the deterioration of fish and seafood (Fig. 2c).36 The \(\left[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}\right]_{n}\) sensor displayed outstanding selectivity, a high response value of 14.1 and a low detection limit of 2 ppm to TMA at a very low operating temperature of \(75\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 2d). Such outstanding performance of the \(\left[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}\right]_{n}\) sensor was attributed to the weak interaction between the TMA molecules and the \(\left[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}\right]_{n}\) framework, providing an ideal candidate for detecting freshness of fish and seafood.
Although ZIFs based on divalent transition-metals have been largely explored,37,56,69 other elements with tetrahedral coordination geometry, such as Li, B and Cu in the periodic table, can also be the choice as tetrahedral nodes to produce MOZs with structural diversity and multifunctionality.70–73
## 2.2 Anionic ZIFs based on the \(\mathbf{M}^{1+}/\mathbf{M}^{2+}\) mixed tetrahedral center
Since monovalent metal centers hardly form MOZs with linkage \(\mathbf{M}^{+}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{Im}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}^{+}\) \(\mathrm{vs}=0.5\), too low), an effective approach to synthesize MOZs is mixing divalent and monovalent metal centers with linkage \(\mathbf{M}^{+}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{Im}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}^{2+}\) \(\mathrm{vs}=0.75\). Based on this idea, Zhang's group developed one kind of anionic ZIFs based on the \(\mathbf{M}^{1+}/\mathbf{M}^{2+}\) mixed tetrahedral center. By mixing \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}/\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) in one system, they successfully synthesized MOZ-1.20 It can be considered as \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)doped ZIF-67, which had a similar SOD-type framework as ZIF-67 except that partial \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) sites were substituted by tetrahedral \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)centers. The average content of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)in MOZ-1 was \(1.71\%\), confirmed by inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES) measurements. Although MOZ-2 had an anionic SOD-type framework, the isolated charge-balancing \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)ions might play the role of active sites for higher gas uptake and catalytic activities. As demonstrated by reversible gas sorption experiments, the Langmuir surface area of MOZ-1 was \(1935.5\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), a value slightly higher than that \((1832.2\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\) of ZIF-67. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) sorptions followed a similar increasing trend, owing to the strong affinity between adsorbates and charge-balanced cations in the
SOD cages. Furthermore, MOZ-1 with a band gap of \(\sim2.2\ \mathrm{eV}\) showed a much higher efficient visible-light photocatalytic activity than ZIF-67 for degradation of methyl orange (MO) in \(\sim25\) minutes because Cu-doping dramatically improved the photocatalytic activity of ZIF-67.
## 2.3 Boron imidazolate frameworks (BIFs)
Inspired by the discovery of microporous \(\mathrm{AlPO}_{4}\) by analogy with porous silica that \(\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{P}^{5+}\) ions can replace two \(\mathrm{Si}^{4+}\) sites in a porous silicalite, by mixing the positive 1 charged metals with the positive 3 charged metals, it will generate a new class of neutral frameworks. By using pre-synthesized \(\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Im})_{4}\) ligands with a \(\mathrm{B}^{3+}\) tetrahedral center to self-assemble with monovalent \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)or \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)or \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\), Prof. Zhang and Bu produced a series of neutral boron imidazolate frameworks (BIFs) with zeolitic topologies.71,74,75 It is notable that both \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)and \(\mathrm{B}^{3+}\) as lightweight elements can help to decrease the density of these materials.76 Similarly to ZIFs, link–link interactions are also important for constructing diverse BIFs. Therefore, some boron-imidazolate ligands with different substituents and connectivity were pre-synthesized to produce BIFs with structural diversity. More details can be seen in a recent review.77
Although most of the BIFs were designed by combining monovalent \(\mathbf{M}^{+}\)and trivalent \(\mathrm{B}^{3+}\) as tetrahedral nodes, it is highly desirable to go beyond \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)and include a much wider variety of metal ions, which might provide access to a broader range of zeolitic topologies. Based on this consideration, a new approach for the synthesis of zeolitic BIFs via the combination of an octahedral divalent metal center and a tetrahedral \(\mathrm{B}^{3+}\) center was established (Fig. 3a). In BIF-22 and BIF-23, the pendent auxiliary acetate (ac) ligands chelated one \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) center to form a distorted tetrahedral \([\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{ac})]^{+}\)unit, which was further linked by another kind of tetrahedral \(\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Im})_{4}\) units to form porous neutral 3D frameworks with ACO and ABW topologies, respectively (Fig. 3b).34 The rational design method from an octahedral metal center to a distorted tetrahedral building node opens a new construction route toward the structural diversity of BIFs.
Furthermore, using dicarboxylate ligands to replace the chelating monocarboxylate ligands could result in pore space partition in the final BIFs (Fig. 4a). By employing flexible adipic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ad}\right)\) and rigid 2,6-naphthalene dicarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{nda}\right)\) as bridging dicarboxylate ligands, two BIFs (BIF-41 and BIF-42) could be obtained and they showed interesting pore space partition inside the host frameworks.35 In BIF-41, \(28\ \mathrm{Im}\) linkers from ten \(\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Im})_{4}\) ligands joined ten \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) nodes to form a distorted GIS-cage with a pore size of \(\sim7\ \mathrm{\AA}\) (Fig. 4b). However, the 8-ring perpendicular to the \(c\)-axis was divided into two 5-rings by the ad ligand that bridged two \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) centers. As a result, the channel along the \(c\)-axis was partitioned by ad ligands into limited cages with a window size of \(3.4 \times 4.5\ \mathrm{\AA}^{2}\), which was much smaller than that \((11 \times 4.5\ \mathrm{\AA}^{2})\) of GIS cages. Being different from the standard GIS net with identical 3D channels, BIF-41 only contained the 1D channels running along the \(b\) axis because the channels in the other directions were partitioned into multiple domains by the bridging ad ligands. In BIF-42, \(\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Im})_{4}\) ligands linked \(\mathrm{Co}\) centers into an ABW-type
In conclusion for this part, directed by the charge matching principle, different kinds of MOZs based on tetrahedral centers
with different charge have been well established. These results reveal that the charge matching principle is powerful for designing MOZs.
## 3. Function-oriented design strategies toward the construction of metal–organic zeolites
According to the charge matching principle, in the \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\)-imidazolate system, it is easy to construct MOZs by using \(\upmu_{2}\)-bridging ligands with negative 1 charge to partly or fully replace the imidazole. Introducing functional groups on such bridge ligands can adjust the pore size and shape, and also can add active sites to improve affinity and selectivity towards certain guest molecules to realize targeted applications. This section will mainly discuss the synthesis of MOZs based on tetrazolate derivatives, isonicotinic acid, chiral imidazolate derivatives, and their diverse potential applications in gas adsorption/separation, enantioselective separation, catalysis, and so on.
## 3.1 MOZs based on tetrazolate ligands
Normally, tetrazole derivatives can adopt a similar \(\upmu_{2}\)-bridging coordination mode as imidazole to link tetrahedral divalent metal centers. For example, 5-substituted tetrazole can adopt 1,4-coordination mode and 1,3-coordination mode to mimic 2- and 4-substituted imidazole. This will increase the possibility for obtaining new MOZs. Of particular interest is that there are two uncoordinated N-heteroatom sites for each \(\upmu_{2}\)-tetrazole ligand, which are potential functional sites for gas sorption. Zhang's group used tetrazolate derivatives to mimic the coordination mode of imidazole and developed a kind of tetrazolate based MOZs. Three isomeric N-rich frameworks (including Ion-type MOZ-2, dia-type MOZ-3 and SOD-type MOZ-5) were synthesized in different solvents, in all of which each \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) site adopted tetrahedral geometry and was connected by four 5-mtz (5-mtz = 5-methyltetrazole) ligands to generate various 4-connected frameworks (Fig. 5). In comparison, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake of SOD-type MOZ-5 was
\(67.6~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) under \(273\,\mathrm{~K~}\) and 1 bar, which was higher than those of 2-methylimidazole-based ZIF-8 \((29.3\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and 3-methyl1,2,4-triazole-based MAF-7 \((62.5\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\) .57 The results suggested that the favorable interaction between adsorbed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules and uncoordinated N atoms from N-rich aromatic rings indeed increased \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake.
Additionally, some bistetrazole ligands can also adopt a similar coordination mode as imidazolate to form MOZs. Lan and coworkers designed a N-rich bistetrazole ligand, 4,5-di(1H- tetrazol-5-yl)-2H-1,2,3-triazole \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{dtt}\right)\) , to prepare the SOD-type IFMC-1, in which the 18-nuclear zinc-cages had a larger pore size of \(6.4\mathrm{~\AA~}\) than \(3.4\mathrm{~\AA~}\) in ZIF-8 (Fig. 6a).41 Zhao and coworkers used another bistetrazole ligand, 1,5-bis(5-tetrazolo)-3-oxapentane \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{btz}\right)\) , to synthesize another SOD-type \(\left[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{btz})\right]\) with 24-nuclear zinc-cages (Fig. 6b).42 In both bistetrazole-based SOD-type MOZs, tetrazole groups adopted a similar coordination mode as imidazolate and exposed abundant uncoordinated nitrogen atoms inside the cages, which were the preferred \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption sites for high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption capacities and adsorption enthalpies (Fig. 6c and d). Under \(273\,\mathrm{K}\) at 1 bar, \(\left[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{btz})\right]\) showed the highest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption capacity of \(35.6\%\) among MOZs because of multipoint interactions between \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and the framework.
## 3.2 MOZs based on tetrazolate/imidazolate ligands
Though some progress has been made in tetrazolate based MOZs, it is still an ongoing challenge to create various MOZs with uncoordinated N-donor sites and rich topologies. Considering the similar coordination mode between imidazole and tetrazole, Zhang's group developed another method by integrating them into one self-assembly system. The merit of this approach is that imidazole can act as the SDA and co-linker to construct the zeotype framework, and tetrazole cooperates in the construction
of the framework and also functionalizes the pore surface by providing uncoordinated N-heteroatom sites (Fig. 7a). One kind of tetrazolate–imidazolate based MOZs with uncoordinated N-heteroatom sites and zeotype topology was developed.23–25 The cooperative assembly of 2-ethylimidazole (2-eim) and 5-mtz ligands in the presence of \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) led to a SOD-type framework, \(Z\mathrm{n}(5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{mtz})\) (2-eim) (MOZ-8) (Fig. 7b), while a RHO-type framework, \(Z\mathrm{n}(5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{mtz})\) (2-pim) (MOZ-9), was developed by replacing 2-eim with 2-propylimidazole (2-pim) under similar reaction conditions (Fig. 6c). As expected, the SOD and RHO topological networks previously known in ZIFs can also be realized by importing tetrazolate ligands. They are stable in common solvents. Remarkably, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake capacity and isosteric heat of
MOZ-8 \((86~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , \(22.6\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})\) ) are also higher than ZIF-8 \((29.3~\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , \(14.9\,\mathrm{~kJ~mol}^{-1})\) with the same SOD topology, showing that the uncoordinated N-heteroatom sites of tetrazole ligands can remarkably optimize \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption. By further changing the substituent groups on Im and tetrazole, other MOZs with different topological nets were also synthesized,25 which suggested a potential approach to generate a large number of zeolite-like frameworks with uncoordinated N-heteroatom sites for optimizing gas sorption.
## 3.3 MOZs based on imidazolate and auxiliary linear ligands
In previously reported MOZs, the organic ligands are always limited to five-ring azole derivatives. In principle, any bridge ligand with negative 1 charge can be introduced in the \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\) -imidazolate system to construct 4-connected frameworks. It might broaden the range of organic ligands and generate a large number of MOZs with unique features and properties.
To keep the tetrahedral and neutral framework features, the auxiliary linkers must be \(\upmu_{2}\) -bridging ligands with negative 1 charge, and it is best for them to have comparative coordination ability and coordination geometry with the Im ligands. Zhang's group developed a new method via introduction of auxiliary uninegative linkers into the zinc–imidazolate system (Fig. 8a). By applying the above approach, three MOZs with isonicotinic acid (Hint) as the auxiliary ligand and with distinct structural topologies (MOZ-13 to MOZ-15) were synthesized.26 In the structure of \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{ad})(\mathrm{int})\) (MOZ-13), each tetrahedral \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) was surrounded by two deprotonated adenine ligands (ad) and two ina ligands, while the multidentate ad ligands adopted a rare \(\upmu_{2}\) -bridged coordination mode completed by its Im ring. \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) sites were connected by ina and ad ligands to generate a new topological network (dmp) which is previously unknown in both zeolites and ZIFs (Fig. 8b). Remarkably, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake
capacity \((108\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\) of the activated MOZ-13 after methanolexchange was higher than that of the currently best performing ZIF-69 \((70\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) of the ZIF series under the same conditions (273 K and 1 bar).79 Dia-type \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{im})_{3}(\mathrm{int})\) (MOZ-14) and neb-type \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{im})(\mathrm{int})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})\) (MOZ-15) can also be obtained by using different Im derivatives.
## 3.4 Homochiral MOZs
Homochiral porous materials, including zeolites and MOFs, are highly desired, because they can combine both shape selectivity and enantioselectivity.80–84 It is difficult to synthesize homochiral zeolites because of their pure inorganic components. In comparison, homochiral MOFs have been obtained from enantio- pure organic ligands and have shown potential applications of heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis and enantioselective separation. However, the preparation of homochiral MOZs, that integrate the zeotype topologies and the homochiral nature, remains highly challenging and is rarely reported. The most reliable approach to prepare homochiral MOZs is to select an enantiopure ligand to impart homochirality into the framework. The low cost and easy synthesis of amino acids and their derivatives make them the best candidates for constructing homochiral MOZs.
A pair of enantiopure amino acids (D- and L-alanine) were selected as linkers to coordinate \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) centers, giving homochiral \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{L}-\mathrm{ala})_{2}\) (MOZ-16L) and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{D}-\mathrm{ala})_{2}\) (MOZ-16D) with zeotype SOD topology, respectively.27 Owing to the unique chelating mode, each alanine anion as a \(\upmu_{2}\) -bridging ligand combined with two 6-coordinate \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) centers which were further reduced as a 4-connecting node, giving a unindical zeotype SOD topology (Fig. 9b). The large steric hindrance between ala ligands and the short Ni···Ni distance of 4.996 Å made it a dense framework without pores.
To obtain porous homochiral MOZs for further applications, tetrazolate derivatives were employed to replace a portion of enantiopure amino acids (Fig. 9a). By choosing L-ala and D-ala in combination with 5-methyltetrazole (5-mtz), Zhang's group
synthesized another pair of homochiral MOZs, \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(5\mathrm{-mtz})_{6}\mathrm{-}\) \(\left(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{ala}\right)_{2}\) (MOZ-17L) and \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(5\mathrm{-mtz})_{6}(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{ala})_{2}\) (MOZ-17D), with ABW topology for the first time.28 Compared to the above SOD-type MOZ-16L, three 5-mtz ligands replaced three L-ala ligands to coordinate one \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) center in the structure of MOZ-17L. All the Zn atoms were linked by 5-mtz ligands to form a layer featured as a 3-connected \((6^{3})\) net, and then L-ala ligands further linked adjacent layers to form a 3D framework (Fig. 9c). The \(Z\mathbf{n}\cdots Z\mathbf{n}\) distances linked by 5-mtz ligands ranged from 6.036 to \(6.227\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , which were much longer than that \((4.996\mathrm{~\AA~})\) in SOD-type MOZ16L. Thus, larger solvent-accessible volumes of \(\sim\!32.1\%\) could be seen in MOZ-17L, leaving large channels with dimensions of ca. \(5.5\,\times\,11.2\,\,\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{2}\) .29 More importantly, they also used other amino acids with different groups, such as L-serine (L-ser) and L-valine (L-val) to form functionalized frameworks \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(5\mathrm{-mtz})_{6}(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{ser})_{2}\) (MOZ-18L) and \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(5\mathrm{-mtz})_{6}(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{val})_{2}\) (MOZ-19L), realizing their chiral recognition of carvone with a modest ee value of \(43.2\%\) .
In another way, imidazolate derivatives with a chiral center on the imidazole ring can be used to construct homochiral MOZs. Although such a simple idea may be known by everybody, homochiral MOZs can be rarely obtained. As proved above, it is a powerful method to construct MOZs by mixing Im and tetrazole derivatives in one system (Fig. 10a). In the following works, two pre-synthesized enantiopure imidazolate derivatives, (S)-2-(1-hydroxyethyl)benzimidazole (D-OH-bim) and (R)-2-(1-hydroxyethyl)benzimidazole (R-OH-bim), in combination with the 5-mtz ligand were used to realize a pair of homochiral zeolitic imidazolaterelated frameworks (MOZ-20R and MOZ-20S) with SOD topology (Fig. 10b).29 A \((4^{6}+8^{5})\) SOD cage consisting of \(24\,\mathrm{Zn}\) atoms, 3-OH-bim ligands and 27 5-mtz ligands is observed (Fig. 1b and d). The four-membered and six-membered rings along other directions are blocked by the big substituents of the OH-bim, just leaving the two six-membered rings along the \(c\) -axis. Then these cages link each other based on the edge-expansion to generate a
SOD-type framework including 1D channels along the \(c\) -axis. Similar to MOZs based on tetrazolate/imidazolate ligands, both 5-mtz and \(R-\) (or S-) OH-bim ligands are \(\upmu_{2}\) -linkers in these frameworks. Differently, the big difference between the 5-mtz and OH-bim ligands reduces the whole symmetry (from cubic to trigonal). They play different roles in the formation of the SOD topology. Without considering the OH-bim ligands, each Zn center is connected by three 5-mtz ligands to form a 3D framework, \([(Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(5\mathrm{-mtz})_{3})]_{n}^{+}\) . Correspondingly, each Zn center acts as a 3-connected node, while the three 5-mtz ligands are classified into two kinds. One is linking the Zn atoms to form \(3_{1}\) helices with both handedness. Another one is linking such helices to form a 3D substructure with the etb net. Based on this result, the 3-connected etb net can be considered as the interrupted SOD net. Moreover, the combined assembly of enantiopure OH-bim, achiral 5-mtz and tetrahedral \(Z\mathbf{n}^{2+}\) ion in the presence of \(\mathrm{HBF_{4}}\) led to a dia-type framework, MOZ-21R (Fig. 10c). These homochiral MOZs also showed permanent porosity and chiral recognition ability.
## 3.5 MOZs based on inorganic and metal–organic tetrahedral units
As mentioned above, ZIFs were developed by mimicking the structural feature of zeolites, which gives a chance to combine them together. Such a combination is trusted to bear the merits of both zeolites and ZIFs, for example, possession of catalytically active \(\mathrm{TO}_{4}\) sites of zeolites and the high porosity of ZIFs (Fig. 11a). Following this idea, Zhang's group reported such kind of hybrid MOZs [MOZ-23, general formula: \(\mathrm{M}_{4}(\mathrm{im})_{6}\mathrm{TO}_{4}]\) with catalytically active \(\mathrm{TO}_{4}\) \(\mathrm{T}=\mathrm{Mo}^{6+}\) or \(\mathbf{W}^{6+}\) ) building blocks and high thermal stability (up to \(550\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , which presents a new class of porous materials filling the gap between zeolites and ZIFs.30
Two iso-structural compounds, MOZ-22Mo and MOZ-22W, were successfully synthesized by the self-assembly of the \(Z\mathbf{n}^{2+}\) cation, \(\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{}^{2-}\) or \(\mathrm{WO}_{4}{}^{2-}\) anions, and 2-mim under solvothermal conditions. The prominent structural feature of this sdt-type framework was to interconnect the truncated octahedral cages of \([\mathrm{Zn}_{24}(2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{mim})_{36}]\) by the inorganic \(\mathrm{MoO_{4}}\) or \(\mathrm{WO_{4}}\) units (Fig. 11b). The large \([\mathrm{Zn}_{24}(2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{mim})_{36}]\) cage with an effective diameter of \(12.5\mathrm{~\AA~}\) and a pore aperture of \(3.3\mathrm{~\AA~}\) in MOZ-22W was just the same as the subunit in ZIF-8. Interestingly, each Zn vertex of this cage was covered by another T \(\left(\mathrm{T}=\mathrm{W}\right)\) node via the Zn–O–T connectivity. Although it was surrounded by inorganic \(\mathrm{WO_{4}}\) units, each window of the cage remained open and faced the next symmetry-related window of the adjacent cage (Fig. 11c). The nodal distance between Zn–O–W was obviously shorter than that between Zn–mim–Zn, while the Zn–O–W angle was bigger than the Zn–mim–Zn angle.
The PXRD patterns below \(550\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) were coincident with the corresponding patterns simulated from single-crystal XRD structures, confirming that the structural and thermal stability of MOZ-22 were significantly greater than those of ZIFs and many other porous MOFs. The remarkable thermal stability and the presence of catalytically active sites gave a chance for the catalytic properties of MOZ-22, which were tested for
selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde and photocatalytic degradation of MO.
Banerjee's group synthesized another kind of hybrid MOZs (MOZIF-1).86 Being different from MOZ-22, each tetrahedral \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) center was occupied by two N atoms from two 2-nitro- \(1H\cdot\) - imidazole \((2{\cdot}\mathrm{nim})\) ligands and two oxygen atoms from two \(\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{}^{2-}\) anions. MOZIF-1 could be considered as a 4-connected zeolitic SOD-type structure. Although the inner diameter of the pore inside MOZIF-1 was \({\sim}10.5\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , a very low BET surface area \((35~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) of MOZIF-1 indicated that there was no significant porosity in the host since all the pore apertures were blocked by the nitro groups of imidazole rings. Due to the presence of the catalytically active tetrahedral \(\mathrm{MoO_{4}}\) unit, MOZIF-1 also showed degradation of MO.
## 3.6 Cluster-based MOZs
Besides single metal ions as tetrahedral centers, some pseudotetrahedral units can also act as candidates to construct MOZs. The development of composite tetrahedral clusters is highly desirable, because of the potential diversity in clusters, large pore size, and the possibility to introduce additional functionality through the construction of clusters. With this method, even elements that usually don't exhibit tetrahedral geometry can be incorporated into the frameworks, thereby creating rich opportunities in structural design and property engineering.
\(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}\) is a neutral and tetrahedral cluster, and exists with four unsaturated \(\mathrm{{Cu}^{+}}\) centers.32 However, the competing crystallization processes of various \(\mathrm{Cu}_{n}\mathrm{I}_{n}\) cluster species make it highly challenging to form zeotype frameworks.87–89 Zhang's group developed a synthetic procedure that demonstrates the feasibility to create zeotype frameworks by using \(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}\) clusters as pseudotetrahedral units (Fig. 12a).31 The typical example was an extraordinary photoluminescent cluster-organic framework based on tetrahedral \(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}\) clusters, namely \([\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}(\mathrm{dabo})_{2}]_{n}\) (MOZ-23; dabco \(=1{,}4\) -diazabicyclo- \(\cdot[2,2,2]\) octane), which featured a zeolite MTN-type framework with two types of giant \(6^{4}5^{12}\) and \(5^{12}\) cages. Remarkably, the larger \(6^{4}5^{12}\) cage in MOZ-23 had an inner diameter of \(2.6\ \mathrm{nm}\) and contained \(28\ \mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}\) clusters as nodes and 42 dabco ligands as linkers (Fig. 12b). There was a very large pore volume of \(9.2\;\mathrm{nm}^{3}\) inside such \(6^{4}5^{12}\) cage. The smaller cage in MOZ-23 was \(5^{12}\) cage which contained \(20\;\;\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}\) clusters and 30 dabco ligands and had an inner diameter of \(2.0\,\textrm{n m}\)
(Fig. 12c). The two giant cages were adjacent to each other, sharing pentagonal windows (Fig. 12d). The solvent-accessible volume of MOZ-23 was \(\sim\!75\%\) of the total crystal volume.85 MOZ-23 also exhibited strong photoluminescent emission under UV light. It revealed perfect integration of porosity and photoluminescent property from both cluster and framework in a porous material. The results further demonstrated that the tetrahedral \(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}\) cluster with bulky size was a very good building unit for the construction of functional cluster-based MOZs.
Liu and coworkers reported another zeolite-like MOZ based on the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}\) cluster, \([(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4})(\mathrm{dabo})_{2}]\cdot[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bbimb})]\) (JLU-Liu23, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bbimb}\;=\;1,3\) -bis(2-benzimidazol)-benzene).43 In JLU-Liu23, the host framework \([\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}(\mathrm{dabo})_{2}]_{n}\) could be described as a three-periodic zeolitic net with four-connected unj topology. Unexpectedly, the nanoporous helical channels running along the \(c\) axis were occupied by unprecedented chiral DNA-like \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bbimb})]_{n}\) polymers with double-helicity, whose diameter and screw-pitch were \(17.2\,\mathring\mathrm{A}\) and \(22.8\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , relatively smaller than
20 and \(34\mathrm{~\AA~}\) observed in the DNA helix, respectively. Multiple \(\mathrm{C-H\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi}\) interactions between the dabco molecules and the benzimidazole rings can also be seen. JLU-Liu23 was the gyroid MOF with homochirality and helical channels in the framework. Importantly, the associated structures based on 23P or 23M could be absolutely promoted and isolated in the presence of appropriate chiral inducing agents,90 affording enantioselective separation of chiral molecules.
In the above cluster-based MOZs, each framework is just assembled from monotonous tetrahedral clusters. Zhang's group also developed heterometallic cluster-organic frameworks with zeotype topologies by combining tetrahedral \(\mathrm{[Cu_{4}I_{4}(i n a)_{4}]^{4-}}\) metalloligands and different 3d-metal clusters together to obtain two compounds, namely \(\mathrm{[(Cu_{4}I_{4})Z n_{8}(\mu_{4}{\cdot}O)(i n a)_{12}(H_{2}O)_{4}]{\cdot}2(C H_{3}C O O)}\) and \(\mathrm{[(Cu_{4}I_{4})M n_{2}(i n a)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}D M F]}\) .88 In the first structure, if considering \(\mu_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}\) as a four-connected node, the 4-connected \(\mathrm{[Cu_{4}I_{4}(i n a)_{4}]^{4-}}\) metalloligands and 3-connected \(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) clusters are linked by \(\mu_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}\) atoms to generate a tetrahedral framework with novel 4-connected topology. In the latter structure, the tetrahedral \(\mathrm{[Cu_{4}I_{4}(i n a)_{4}]^{4-}}\) metalloligands linked distorted tetrahedral \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})\) units to form a 2-fold interpenetrating dia-type framework. These cluster-organic framework materials can serve as efficient heterogeneous catalysts for the addition reaction of diethylzinc to aromatic aldehydes. Although both structures are not zeolite-type nets, the result is instructive to synthesize cluster-based MOZs combining two different tetrahedral units.
Typified by dense silica polymorphs and zeolites, polyoxometalates (POMs) with diverse coordination modes have also been regarded as tetrahedral building units for the design of zeolitic POM-base metal–organic frameworks (Z-POMOFs) with a large variety of organic linkers. With the aim of assessing the viability of POM-based tetrahedral nets, Rabdel and coworkers made great efforts in this area.44,91 In theory, Z-POMOFs made of isolated e-keggin \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) -capped \(\mathrm{\varepsilon-PMo_{8}^{V}M o_{4}^{V I}O_{36^{-}}(O H)_{4}Z n_{4}}\) (e-Zn POM) building-blocks connected through \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) (benzene1,4-dicarboxylic acid) can crystallize in 21 zeotype structures simulated by computational exploration. However, the first experimental Z-POMOF with the chemical formula \([\mathrm{NBu}_{4}]_{3}.\) - \(\mathrm{\small{[PMo_{8}^{V}M o_{4}^{V I}O_{36}(O H)_{4}Z n_{4}-(B D C)_{2}]{\cdot}2H_{2}O}}\) (Z-POMOF-1) was identified as a 3-fold dia-type net with a stable structure.44 In their continued work, given that the \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{M-Im-M}\) angle of \(145^{\circ}\) was close to typical Si–O–Si angles in siliceous zeolites, the Im ligand was considered for replacing the position of the BDC ligand. However, a 2D material \([\mathrm{NBu}_{4}][\mathrm{PMo}_{8}^{\mathrm{V}}\mathrm{Mo}_{4}^{\mathrm{VIO}_{36}}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}\) \(\left(\mathrm{HIm}\right)]\) was obtained.91
Recently, inspired by Rabdel's work, Lan believed that the metal–ligand–metal angle similar to the Si–O–Si angle in zeolites and the moderate length of the linker were two critical parameters to build Z-POMOFs.45 They finally chose the \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) -capped \(\varepsilon{-}\{\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{PMo}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\}\) cluster as the tetrahedral node, Hbzt (benzotriazole) as the ligand providing a metal–ligand–metal angle of \(145^{\circ}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}\) with a moderate length as the secondary bridging ligand to successfully synthesize the first Z-POMOF with an ABWtype topological structure (NENU-601). In the \(\varepsilon{-}\{\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{PMo}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\}\) cluster, three quarters of peripheral \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) atoms were coordinated
by three \(\mathrm{bzt}^{-}\) ligands, which showed the 1,3-coordination mode to mimic imidazole. Meanwhile, the remaining \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) atom was combined by an O atom from the \(\mathrm{bdc}^{2-}\) ligand. Tetrahedral connections of these \(\varepsilon{-}\{\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{PMo}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\}\) clusters were linked by \(\mathrm{bzt}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{bdc}^{2-}\) ligands to generate the final porous MENU-601 with ABW-type topology. MENU-601 kept its crystalline integrity in aqueous solution in a range of \(\mathrm{pH}\) values from 1 to 12 and held its skeleton intact up to \(370\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) showing its very high chemical and thermal stability. They directly utilized MENU-601 as an anode material for lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) because of its superior chemical and structural stability. MENU-601 exhibited a high reversible capacity of \(470\ \mathrm{mA\h\g^{-1}}\) after 200 cycles at a current density of \(500\,\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}\) , showing its extraordinary performance for application in LIBs.
## 3.7 MOZs based on carboxylates
Based on the single-metal-ion molecular building block (MBB) approach that possessed both desired angularities and heterofunctionality, imidazole-dicarboxylates and pyrimidinecarboxylates represent potential attractive candidates for synthesizing MOZs. Eddaoudi employed \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) cations and 4,5-imidazoledicarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{ImDC}\right)\) , which possessed two N-,O-hetero-chelating moieties with a potential angle of \(144^{\circ}\) (directed by the M–N coordination), to construct MOZs with zeolitic topologies.8,46 In these MOZs, although each indium metal ion was coordinated to four nitrogen atoms and four oxygen atoms of four separate HimDC ligands, to form an eight-coordinate MBB, \(\mathrm{InN}_{4}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C})_{4}\) , the final topologies of these anionic In-ImDC frameworks were highly dependent on cationic SDAs. Specifically, imidazole (HIm) led to SOD-ZMOF-1, while 1,3,4,6,7,8-hexahydro-2H-pyrimido[1,2- a]pyrimidine (HPP) yielded RHO-ZMOF-1.46 1,2-Diaminocyclohexane \(\left(1,2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DACH}\right)\) as the SDA gave rise to the formation of usf-ZMOF with med topology.8 Especially for RHO-ZMOF-1, its anionic and porous features demonstrate the feasibility of enclosing a cationic fluorophore, protonated acridine orange (AO), as a probe for detecting methyl xanthines and DNA nucleoside bases,46 \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) and \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) -exchange for optimizing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) sorption energetics and uptake10 and encapsulating free base porphyrin \(\mathrm{[H_{2}T M P y P][p-t o s y l]^{4+}}\) for cyclohexane oxidation.92
Over 76 zeolite structures were constituted by a four-membered ring (4R) as the secondary building unit (SBU). Thus, new functional zeolite-like supramolecular assemblies (ZSAs) can be synthesized via the supramolecular assembly (e.g., hydrogen bonding) of metal–organic squares (MOSs) containing complementary peripheral functional groups. In 2010, by using 4,5-imidazoledicarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{ImDC}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) Liu and Eddaoudi reported a novel porous zeolite-like supramolecular assembly (ZSA-1) with GIS topology, in which four \({\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}\) ions were combined by four 1,2-propanediamine (1,2-PDA) terminal ligands and four bridging \(\mathrm{ImDC}^{3-}\) ligands to generate a 4R-based MOS. Each 4R-based MOS connected four neighboring squares through 16 hydrogen bond interactions to form ladderlike chains via edge-to-edge intermolecular N–H···O hydrogen bonds, generating the final GIS-type supramolecular framework.47 Moreover, the highly stable ZSA-1 exhibited an excellent methane storage capacity of \(140\;\mathrm{v/v}\) (STP) at room temperature
and 35 bar. Upon further structural analysis, they found that the orientation of the ligands pointed to the pores; thus, the properties of ZSA materials could be further modulated by introducing Lewis basic site (LBS, including \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), \(-\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}\) and \(-\mathrm{Pr}\)) functional groups into 2 position in ImDC of ZSA-1 to tune its sorption and separation abilities, constructing three isoreticular materials \(\left[\mathrm{Co}_{4}(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-ImDC})_{4}(1,2\mathrm{-PDA})_{4}\right]\cdot8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (ZSA-7), \(\left[\mathrm{Co}_{4}(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}\mathrm{-ImDC})_{4}(1,2\mathrm{-PDA})_{4}\right]\cdot8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (ZSA-8), and \(\left[\mathrm{Co}_{4}(\mathrm{Pr}\mathrm{-ImDC})_{4}(1,2\mathrm{-PDA})_{4}\right]\cdot9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (ZSA-9). As expected, these three ZSA materials possessed high thermal stability and permanent porosity similar to ZSA-1. However, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake \((86.3\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for ZSA-7 and \(91.5\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for ZSA-8 under \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) and 1 bar), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption enthalpies \((41.7\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) for ZSA-7 and \(41.5\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) for ZSA-8), and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity (39.8 for ZSA-7 and 35.7 for ZSA-8 for an equimolar mixture at 1 bar and \(298\;\mathrm{K}\)) for LBS-functionalized ZSA-7 and ZSA-8 are much higher than for the alkyl modified ZSA-9.
Although "pure" carboxylates (those with only \(\mathrm{COO}^{-}\) donor groups) have permeated most aspects of MOF chemistry, they just play a minor role in the design of MOZs. Actually, some special zeolite-type nets can accommodate a large range of T–O–T angles. Being inspired by zinc arsenate sodalite with increasing T–O bond length and a low T–O–T angle of \(123.8^{\circ}\), Zheng and Bu selected \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) ions as metal centers and mixed dicarboxylic linkers aip \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{aip}\right)=5\)-aminoisophthalic acid) with \(120^{\circ}\) and bpdc \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpcd}=4,4^{\prime}\right.\)-biphenyldicarboxylic acid) with \(180^{\circ}\) angles as complementary and cooperative linkers for the construction of three-ring-based MOZ, denoted as \(\mathrm{CPM-2-NH}_{2}\) (CPM = crystalline porous material), can form a four-connected \(\left\{\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{4}\right\}\) group, which is an appealing T node for targeting MOZs. Isophthalic acid derivatives with \(120^{\circ}\) bend angle possess the ability to induce the assembly of triangular \(\left\{\mathrm{In}_{3}(\mathrm{aip})_{3}\right\}\) three-rings. The three-ring channel consists of one kind of nonanuclear \(\left\{\mathrm{In}_{3}(\mathrm{aip})_{6}(\mathrm{bpcd})_{6}\right\}\) cage built from two \(\left\{\mathrm{In}_{3}(\mathrm{aip})_{3}\right\}\) three-rings linked by three \(\left\{\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{bpcd})_{2}\right\}\) groups. By sharing three-ring faces, adjacent cages are joined together to form infinite three-ring channels. Interestingly, arrays of three-ring channels form a hexagonal pattern and are interconnected through linear bpdc linkers to generate a rare 12-ring channel, resulting in the formation of a three-fold interweaving structure in CPM-2 with MTN topology. By using two other bent dicarboxylates (i.e., \(5\mathrm{-H}_{2}\mathrm{hip}=5\)-hydroxyisophthalic acid; \(1,3\mathrm{-H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}=1,3\)-benzenedicarboxylic acid), two more phases (denoted as CPM-2-OH and CPM-2-H) were obtained. \(\mathrm{CPM-2-NH}_{2}\) retained its crystallinity up to about \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and showed its band gap of \(3.0\;\mathrm{eV}\). For \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production experiments, \(1\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) Pt-loaded CPM-2-\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) exhibited a stable \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) release rate of \(2.0\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) per \(0.1\;\mathrm{g}\) under the irradiation of \(500\;\mathrm{W}\) UV.
Based on the net-to-net assembly strategy, understanding the relation between zeolitic topologies and their sub-networks is the key point to obtain MOZs with defined topologies. And then, according to coordination chemistry theory, sub-networks can be consciously assembled into predicted topologies. Recently, Yuan's group developed a new approach toward MOZs via introduction of additional tetrahedral nodes into the assembly of interpenetrating dia-type frameworks. In the structure of
\(\left[\mathrm{InCu}(\mathrm{bpydc})_{2}\right]_{n}\) (MOZ-25, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpydc}=2,2\)-bipyridine-5,5-dicarboxylic acid), all \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\) units were linked by bpydc ligands to form an anionic 4-fold interpenetrating dia framework, in which two adjacent dia nets were further tetrahedrally crosslinked by \(\left[\mathrm{CuN}_{4}\right]^{+}\)ions into the final 2-fold interpenetrating GIS-type MOZ-25 (Fig. 13a). Compared to the common 4-fold anionic dia-type nets, such a net-to-net assembly process provided some advantages for the 2-fold neutral GIS-type framework, including more preferable structural stability (upon \(280\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)), a moderate aperture of \(6.7\;\mathrm{\AA}\), accessible volume and higher \(\mathrm{OMSs}(\mathrm{Cu}^{+})\) density of \(2.28\;\mathrm{OMSs}(\mathrm{Cu}^{+})/\mathrm{nm}^{3}\) for optimizing gas sorption. The activated sample of MOZ-25 showed superior adsorption of \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\) \((250.1\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) \((232.5\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) \((147.8\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) (Fig. 13b and c). Particularly, the safe acetylene storage density for MOZ-25 is \(0.17\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) under ambient conditions, being equivalent to 78 times the compression limit for the safe storage of acetylene \((0.2\;\mathrm{MPa})\). Furthermore, \(n\mathrm{-C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{10}/\mathrm{i}\mathrm{-C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{10}\) adsorption selectivity in excess of 84 for MOZ-25 indicated its outstanding sieving properties for \(n\mathrm{-C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{10}/\mathrm{i}\mathrm{-C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{10}\) separation (Fig. 13c).
## 4. Conclusions and outlook
Based on the current societal and industrial demand for functional porous materials for targeted applications, this review focuses on identifying possible strategies and relevant examples of MOZs that possess periodic porous frameworks with zeolitic topologies and properties. We highlight the basic principles and classify the possible strategies for designing MOZs. The presented strategies offer great potential to construct new kinds of MOZs. For example, directed by the charge balancing principle, tetrahedral centers with different charge can be introduced to develop a diversity of MOZs; introducing the ligands with functional groups can extend
the potential applications of MOZs; and enlarging nodes from tetrahedral clusters, as well as expanding the components of MOZs from the traditional imidazole-linked ligands to organic carboxylate ligands can diversify the structures of MOZs.
MOZs as solid porous materials allow for diverse application fields where traditional zeolites are limited. In fact, innovative functions are specifically associated with their porous nature. Most concerted efforts highlight that the gas sorption/separation ability (especially for selective storage and separation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\)) can be optimized in the presence of polar groups, uncoordinated N-heteroatom sites and accessible open metal sites. Recent results demonstrate the ability of MOZs for enantiomeric separation by introducing a chiral center into host frameworks, as well as support their catalytic activity mediated by the replacement of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)or \(\mathrm{TO}_{4}^{2-}\). The presence of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}\) and polyoxometalate clusters in cluster-based MOZs respectively endow them with exceptional fluorescence and electrochemistry properties.
Presently, the research on the synthesis and potential properties of MOZs with novel structures has made remarkable achievements, which will provide experience and strategic guidance for further research on MOZs. Nonetheless, the actual synthetic and applied processes still have a long way to go. There are some issues that should be taken into account in the future research focused on developing new applications of either already documented or newly designed MOZs, such as (1) MOZs with richer topologies and better performance should be synthesized through rational assembly of tetrahedral centers/clusters and ligands; (2) the applications of MOZs should be deeply studied in the future.
